WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.884
In this video, we'll
focus on checking outputs

2
00:00:07.884 --> 00:00:09.286
generated by the system.

3
00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.917
Checking outputs before
showing them to users can be

4
00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:15.540
important for ensuring the quality,
relevance and safety

5
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.145
of the responses provided to them
or used in automation flows.

6
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:22.198
We'll
learn how to use the moderation API, but

7
00:00:22.198 --> 00:00:24.630
this time for outputs, and
how to use additional

8
00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.792
prompts to the model
to evaluate output quality

9
00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:27.602
before displaying them.

10
00:00:28.724 --> 00:00:30.207
So, let's dive into the examples.

11
00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:34.122
We've already discussed the moderation
API in the context

12
00:00:34.122 --> 00:00:37.647
of evaluating inputs. Now, let's
revisit it in the context

13
00:00:37.647 --> 00:00:38.704
of checking outputs.

14
00:00:39.466 --> 00:00:42.169
Moderation API can also
be used to filter

15
00:00:42.169 --> 00:00:45.885
and moderate outputs generated
by the system itself. And so, here's

16
00:00:45.885 --> 00:00:46.560
an example.

17
00:00:47.743 --> 00:00:52.690
So, here's
a generated response to the user.

18
00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:52.690


19
00:00:54.436 --> 00:00:57.115
And we're going to use the
moderation API in the

20
00:00:57.115 --> 00:00:59.526
same way that we saw in the earlier
video.

21
00:01:02.111 --> 00:01:04.770
So, let's
see if this output is flagged.

22
00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.563
And as you can see,
this output is not

23
00:01:09.563 --> 00:01:12.866
flagged and has very
low scores in all categories,

24
00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:17.270
which makes sense given the response.
In general, it can also be

25
00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.105
important to check the outputs.

26
00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:24.277
For example, if you were creating
a chatbot for sensitive audiences,

27
00:01:24.277 --> 00:01:27.542
you could use lower
thresholds for flagging outputs.

28
00:01:28.164 --> 00:01:31.111
In general, if the moderation
output indicates that

29
00:01:31.111 --> 00:01:34.796
the content is flagged, you can take
appropriate action such

30
00:01:34.796 --> 00:01:38.113
as responding with
a fallback answer or generating a

31
00:01:38.113 --> 00:01:38.850
new response.

32
00:01:40.087 --> 00:01:44.386
Note that as we improve the models,
they also are becoming less and

33
00:01:44.386 --> 00:01:47.362
less likely to return some kind
of harmful output.

34
00:01:47.362 --> 00:01:50.338
Another approach for
checking outputs is to ask the

35
00:01:50.338 --> 00:01:54.306
model itself if the generated was
satisfactory and if it follows a

36
00:01:54.306 --> 00:01:55.959
certain rubric that you define.

37
00:01:56.641 --> 00:01:58.717
This can be done
by providing the generated

38
00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:01.832
output as part of the input to the
model and asking it

39
00:02:01.832 --> 00:02:03.650
to rate the quality of the output.

40
00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:07.478
You can do this
in various different ways. So, let's

41
00:02:07.478 --> 00:02:08.144
see an example.

42
00:02:09.927 --> 00:02:14.635
So, our system message is,
"You are an assistant that evaluates

43
00:02:14.635 --> 00:02:17.203
whether customer service
agent responses sufficiently

44
00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:21.054
answer customer questions
and also validates that all the

45
00:02:21.054 --> 00:02:25.334
facts the assistant cites from the
product information are correct.

46
00:02:25.334 --> 00:02:29.186
The product information
and user and customer service agent

47
00:02:29.186 --> 00:02:34.321
messages will be delivered by three
backticks. Respond with a Y or

48
00:02:34.321 --> 00:02:37.745
N character with no punctuation.
Y if the

49
00:02:37.745 --> 00:02:40.313
output sufficiently answers
the question and

50
00:02:40.313 --> 00:02:42.880
the response correctly
uses product information

51
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.304
and N otherwise.
Output a single letter only.".

52
00:02:47.005 --> 00:02:50.734
And you could also use a chain of
thought reasoning prompt for this.

53
00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:53.028
This might be a
little bit difficult for

54
00:02:53.028 --> 00:02:56.470
the model to validate
both in one step so you could play

55
00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.912
around with this. You could also add
some other kind of guidelines.

56
00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:03.066
You could ask, give a rubric,
like a rubric for an

57
00:03:03.066 --> 00:03:04.500
exam or grading an essay.

58
00:03:05.462 --> 00:03:08.159
You could use that
kind of format and say,

59
00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:11.754
does this use a friendly tone
in line with our brand guidelines

60
00:03:11.754 --> 00:03:14.750
and maybe outline some of your
brand guidelines if that's

61
00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:17.747
something that's very
important to you. So let's add our

62
00:03:17.747 --> 00:03:20.443
customer message. So this is
the initial message used

63
00:03:20.443 --> 00:03:23.439
to generate this response
and then let's also paste in

64
00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:27.334
our product information. And so this is
the product information we fetched in

65
00:03:27.334 --> 00:03:30.031
the previous step
for all of the products mentioned

66
00:03:30.031 --> 00:03:30.930
in this message.


67
00:03:33.919 --> 00:03:39.100
And now we'll define our comparison. So
the customer message is

68
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.396
the customer message, the
product information, and

69
00:03:42.396 --> 00:03:46.635
then the agent response, which
is the response to

70
00:03:46.635 --> 00:03:50.873
the customer that we
have from this previous cell.

71
00:03:51.835 --> 00:03:54.965
So let's format this into a
messages list and get

72
00:03:54.965 --> 00:03:56.530
the response from the model.

73
00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.436
So the model says, yes,
the product information

74
00:04:02.436 --> 00:04:05.583
is correct and the question is
answered sufficiently.

75
00:04:06.464 --> 00:04:09.596
Well, in general,
for these kind of evaluation tasks,

76
00:04:09.596 --> 00:04:14.466
I also think it is better to use
a more advanced model because they're

77
00:04:14.466 --> 00:04:17.250
just better at reasoning. So
something like GPT-4.

78
00:04:20.092 --> 00:04:21.970
Let's try another example.

79
00:04:23.639 --> 00:04:26.765
So this response is,
"life is like a box of chocolates".

80
00:04:27.927 --> 00:04:31.090
So let's add our message to do
with the output checking.

81
00:04:36.204 --> 00:04:39.481
And the model has determined that
this does not

82
00:04:39.481 --> 00:04:41.665
sufficiently answer the
question or use

83
00:04:41.665 --> 00:04:42.757
the retrieved information.

84
00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:47.347
This question, "does it use
the retrieved information correctly?".

85
00:04:47.347 --> 00:04:51.156
This is a good prompt
to use if you want

86
00:04:51.156 --> 00:04:54.583
to make sure that the model isn't
hallucinating, which

87
00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:57.250
is making up things that aren't true.


88
00:05:00.012 --> 00:05:03.903
And feel free to pause
the video now and try

89
00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.627
some of your own customer messages,
responses,

90
00:05:06.627 --> 00:05:10.130
and adding product information
to test how this works.

91
00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.841
So as you can see,
the model can provide

92
00:05:13.841 --> 00:05:15.798
feedback on the quality
of a generated output,

93
00:05:15.798 --> 00:05:17.755
and you can use
this feedback to decide

94
00:05:17.755 --> 00:05:20.691
whether to present the
output to the user or to generate a

95
00:05:20.691 --> 00:05:22.648
new response. You could even
experiment with generating

96
00:05:22.648 --> 00:05:24.850
multiple model responses
per user query and then having

97
00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:27.785
the model choose the best one
to show the user. So there's

98
00:05:27.785 --> 00:05:29.498
lots of different things
you could try.

99
00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:32.076
In general,
checking outputs using the

100
00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:35.736
moderation API is good practice,
but while asking the model to

101
00:05:35.736 --> 00:05:38.731
evaluate its own output might be
useful for immediate

102
00:05:38.731 --> 00:05:42.724
feedback to ensure the quality of
responses in a very small number

103
00:05:42.724 --> 00:05:43.389
of cases.

104
00:05:43.951 --> 00:05:47.095
I think it's
probably unnecessary most of the time, especially

105
00:05:47.095 --> 00:05:49.924
if you're using
a more advanced model like GPT-4.

106
00:05:49.924 --> 00:05:49.924


107
00:05:50.505 --> 00:05:53.938
I haven't actually seen many
people do something like this in

108
00:05:53.938 --> 00:05:56.123
production. It
would also increase the latency

109
00:05:56.123 --> 00:06:00.179
and cost of your system, because
you'd have to wait for an additional

110
00:06:00.179 --> 00:06:03.924
call for the model, and that's
also additional tokens. If it's really

111
00:06:03.924 --> 00:06:07.045
important for your app or product
that your error rate

112
00:06:07.045 --> 00:06:11.101
is 0.0000001%, then maybe you should try
this approach. But overall, I wouldn't

113
00:06:11.101 --> 00:06:13.910
really recommend that you do
this in practice. In

114
00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:17.031
the next video, we're going
to put together everything we've

115
00:06:17.031 --> 00:06:21.087
learned in the evaluate input section, process
section, and checking output section to

116
00:06:21.087 --> 00:06:22.336
build an end-to-end
system.

117
